Risks
Too Many Layers
Risk: the map becomes incomprehensible.
Mitigation: each scenario foregrounds two or three layers; the rest become modifiers or unlock later.
Moral Flattening
Risk: corporations become pure villains and rebels pure heroes.
Mitigation: maintained systems save lives; sabotage can kill; rebels may be right and reckless; corporate operators may be sincere and coercive.
Africa as Victim-Zone
Risk: West Africa / Sahel becomes a passive suffering backdrop.
Mitigation: emphasize local states, activists, courts, youth movements, urban power, culture, corporate collaborators, Free-Net institutions, Champions, and internal disagreements.
White Sky Science Over Gameplay
Risk: climate modeling overwhelms the player.
Mitigation: convert science into readable clocks, overlays, disputed claims, and specific objectives.
Global Scale Too Early
Risk: campaign simulates the entire planet before local stakes work.
Mitigation: start with one receiver-port/rainfall region and imply global echoes through events and diplomacy.
Maintenance Binary
Risk: maintained good / sacrificed bad.
Mitigation: maintained means controlled; sacrificed means abandoned but alive; contested means possibility.
Sabotage as Automatically Evil
Risk: choices become obvious if sabotage always causes mass death.
Mitigation: distinguish exposure, selective disruption, spoofing, redundancy discovery, delayed clocks, and evacuation preparation.
Lore-Only Drift
Risk: bridge becomes a world essay instead of design material.
Mitigation: always translate back into map objects, resources, clocks, hero roles, victory conditions, and UI layers.
Decisions Needed


What is the first campaign region?

Recommendation: Lagos / Gulf of Guinea receiver-port corridor with inland rainfall attribution links.



What is the default visible map layer?

Recommendation: maintenance status first, with national borders visible underneath.



How many resources in the prototype?

Recommendation: Energy, Compute, Logistics, Legitimacy, Proof, Climate Stability.



How many crisis clocks per scenario?

Recommendation: three active clocks.



Is Blue Week global, regional, or scattered?

Recommendation: scattered global phenomenon; first campaign studies one regional consequence.



What caused Blue Week?

Recommendation: eco-anarchist / Open Sky action is real, but it triggers or reveals other failures. Several explanations can be partly true.



Are Meridian Stations capturable map sites?

Recommendation: yes, but not all by combat. Some are treaty, exposure, engineering, or legitimacy objectives.



Can the player intentionally trigger Blue Breaks?

Recommendation: eventually yes, but early campaign should make this dangerous and politically costly.



Are corporate-aligned stabilizers playable?

Recommendation: yes or at least semi-playable. Without this, the central moral dilemma weakens.



Are states meaningful actors or scenery?

Recommendation: meaningful but constrained. States provide legitimacy, armies, courts, identity, and national myths; corporations provide continuity services; neither fully rules alone.



Can sacrificed territories be improved?

Recommendation: yes, but improvement requires choosing a maintenance model, not just spending resources.



What does winning mean?

Recommendation: forcing a governance settlement under crisis, not saving the world absolutely.

locations/kantograd.content:
Kantograd
One-Line Summary
Kantograd is the post-Baltic-War free city created from Kaliningrad: a demilitarized Baltic port, philosophical-symbolic humiliation, smuggling hub, arbitration zone, intelligence nest, and legally overdetermined borderland.
Canon Status
Soft-canon seed. The user-approved direction is that Kaliningrad is renamed Kantograd, after Immanuel Kant, and operated as a free city in the broad tradition of Danzig.
What It Is
Kantograd occupies the former Kaliningrad exclave. After the Baltic War, the territory could no longer function as Russia’s fortified Baltic dagger without making the peace settlement meaningless. But annexing it outright was too dangerous, and expelling Russia from every symbolic claim risked nuclear escalation and permanent revanchism.
The compromise was the Kantograd Charter.
Kantograd is demilitarized, internationally guaranteed, commercially open, heavily monitored, and legally strange. Russia retains a residual symbolic relationship. Europe controls much of the inspection, port, customs, and security environment. Local authorities survive by playing every guarantor, investor, smuggler, and intelligence service against each other.
Why “Kantograd”
The name is a deliberate postwar instrument.
It invokes Immanuel Kant, the famous Königsberg resident, and lets the settlement speak in the language of law, reason, peace, and European civilization rather than conquest. It also humiliates Moscow: a militarized Russian exclave is converted into a city named for a philosopher of perpetual peace and constrained sovereignty.
Different groups hear the name differently:

European lawyers hear a peace settlement.
Russian nationalists hear an insult.
locals hear a rebrand imposed by outsiders.
smugglers hear opportunity.
philosophers and feed aesthetes hear a marketable myth.

Political Status
Kantograd is not cleanly sovereign.
Possible charter features:

demilitarized free city;
elected municipal government under treaty constraints;
European inspection rights;
Russian cultural and consular guarantees;
port neutrality provisions;
no heavy weapons or foreign bases;
special arbitration courts;
customs privileges;
strict limits on military logistics;
permanent monitoring by treaty authorities and private contractors.

The city is peaceful because everyone is watching it. The city is dangerous because everyone is watching it.
Lived Texture
Kantograd should feel grounded rather than flashy:

old Prussian, Soviet, Russian, Polish, Lithuanian, German, and EU layers pressed into one port city;
clean treaty districts beside gray smuggling docks;
philosophy museums sponsored by security firms;
arbitration offices in restored old buildings;
Russian exile cafes under European compliance signage;
Baltic sailors, Ukrainian contractors, German insurers, Polish customs officers, Russian agents, and corporate lawyers in the same rainless white light;
private security with unclear jurisdiction;
feed tours selling “the city where Europe chose law over revenge.”

Strategic Role
Kantograd is valuable because it sits at the intersection of:

Baltic shipping;
sanctions evasion;
Russian-European backchannels;
military inspection politics;
insurance arbitration;
exile finance;
private-security contracting;
drone and counter-drone smuggling;
memory/feed authenticity disputes;
postwar propaganda.

It can host campaigns about treaty violations, assassinations, defectors, black-market Ukrainian defense software, Russian revenge cults, or corporate attempts to buy parts of the city through port concessions.
Relationship to Russia
Russia officially treats Kantograd as an imposed compromise and a wound that has not healed. Russian media alternates between promising eventual restoration, denouncing the city as occupied, and quietly using it for trade and deniable diplomacy.
For Moscow, Kantograd is useful and intolerable at the same time.
Relationship to Europe and Ukraine
Europe treats Kantograd as proof that law can tame geography. Ukraine views it with colder eyes: useful, necessary, but dangerously soft on Russian imperial memory.
Ukrainian companies and veterans are present in Kantograd’s security market, especially through systems linked to Vezha Systems. Their presence gives the city a second postwar charge: the former Russian fortress now buys survival software from the country Russia failed to break.
Open Questions

Is Kantograd formally under UN, European Security Compact, or bespoke treaty supervision?
Does Russia retain nominal sovereignty, cultural guardianship, or only consular guarantees?
What is the local identity: Kantograder, Königsberger, Kaliningrader, Baltic, Russian, European, or opportunistic?
Which faction, company, or Champion controls the port security contracts?
Is Kantograd beautiful, rotten, or both?

